// ============================================================================
== 1.1 The Eight Word Classes

Every English sentence is built from eight kinds of word, and the whole of the lexis and structure section of the paper rests on telling them apart. The good news is that the classes are not a list to be memorised in the abstract: a word's class is the *job it is doing in that sentence*, and the job can be found by asking three questions.

#diagram-fig("diagrams/ch01_class_map.svg",
  "The eight word classes with the job each one does. The examples matter more than the definitions: the paper almost always asks you to sort a word from a sentence, not to recite a definition.", width: 100%)

#concept-box("The three tests that identify a class")[
  + *The article test.* If the word can follow *a*, *an* or *the*, it is a *noun*: a *book*, the *truth*, an *idea*.
  + *The -ly test.* If the word can be made from an adjective by adding -ly and it then describes how something is done, it is an *adverb*: quick → quickly, careful → carefully. The test runs in one direction only — not every word ending in -ly is an adverb.
  + *The position test.* A word between the article and the noun is an *adjective* (the *tall* boy); a word after the verb that tells you how, when or where is an *adverb* (he ran *quickly*).
]

These three tests answer most of the questions in this chapter. Where they fail, the reason is almost always that the word has shifted class — and that shift is the favourite trick of the examiner, because it punishes the candidate who learned the word rather than the sentence.

*Why the paper cares.* A JAMB sentence-improvement question gives you a sentence with one word wrong and four ways of replacing it. To choose, you have to know what the wrong word was trying to be. “The boy behaved very *bad*” is wrong because the verb *behaved* needs an adverb, not an adjective; “he is a *careful* driver” is right because *careful* describes the noun *driver*. The class decides the form, and the form decides the answer.

// ============================================================================
== 1.2 Nouns and Their Jobs

A noun names a person, a place, a thing, a quality or an idea. That is the definition, and it is worth almost nothing on its own, because the questions are about the *kind* of noun and the *form* it takes in the plural and the possessive.

#diagram-fig("diagrams/ch01_noun_types.svg",
  "Six kinds of noun. The mass nouns in the last card are the ones the paper tests, and they are the ones candidates most often give a plural.", width: 100%)

#rule-box("Plurals: the rules the paper uses")[
  + Words ending in *s, x, ch, sh* add *-es*: buses, boxes, churches, brushes.
  + A consonant followed by *y* changes to *-ies*: city → cities, lady → ladies. A vowel before the *y* keeps it: boy → boys, day → days.
  + Most words ending in *f* or *fe* change to *-ves*: leaf → leaves, knife → knives, wife → wives. Exceptions worth knowing: roofs, chiefs, proofs.
  + Irregular plurals: man → men, child → children, mouse → mice, tooth → teeth, foot → feet.
  + Words borrowed from Latin and Greek keep their own plurals: phenomenon → phenomena, criterion → criteria, analysis → analyses, crisis → crises, datum → data, medium → media.

  *Mass nouns never take a plural at all*: advice, furniture, equipment, information, luggage, machinery, baggage, news, poetry, scenery. They take *much* rather than *many*, and they never follow *a* or *an*.
]

*The possessive.* Singular nouns add *'s* (the boy's book, James's pen); plural nouns already ending in *-s* add only the apostrophe (the boys' books); irregular plurals add *'s* (the children's toys). The test is where the apostrophe sits: it always comes *after* the owner, and the owner is whatever possesses the thing that follows.

#worked-example(
  1,
  "Choosing between the noun and the verb form",
  [Complete the sentence: “The teacher gave us some useful \_\_\_.” Options: advice, advices, advise, advises.],
  [
    *Step 1 — decide what kind of word the blank needs.* It follows *some useful*, an article-plus-adjective frame, so the blank is a noun. That removes *advise* and *advises*, which are verbs.

    *Step 2 — decide the number.* *Advice* is a mass noun: it names an amount of something that is not counted in units, like *information* or *furniture*. Mass nouns have no plural, so *advices* does not exist in standard English.

    *Step 3 — test the sentence.* “Some useful advice” reads correctly; “some useful advices” does not, and the reason is not style but grammar.

    *Check.* Change the determiner to *much*: “much useful advice” works, while “many useful advice” does not — and that is the signature of a mass noun. Every mass noun in the list above passes the same test.
  ],
  tier: "FOUNDATION",
)

#trap-box("Advices, furnitures and informations")[
  The paper offers these forms as options every year, and they are always wrong. The mass nouns are *advice, furniture, equipment, information, luggage, baggage, machinery, poetry, scenery, news, work* (in the sense of employment). To count them, use a partitive: *a piece of advice*, *an item of furniture*, *a piece of equipment*, *a bit of information*.
]

#worked-example(
  2,
  "The plural that looks regular and is not",
  [“The \_\_\_ in the laboratory were replaced last term.” Options: apparatus, apparatuses, apparatus, apparatus's.],
  [
    *Step 1 — the verb tells you the number.* *Were* is plural, so the subject must be plural.

    *Step 2 — check what the noun is.* *Apparatus* is a fourth-declension Latin noun that looks plural and behaves as singular in British usage: *the apparatus is*. Its plural is *apparatuses* or, in scientific writing, *apparatus* used with a plural verb.

    *Step 3 — pick the option the examiner wants.* *Apparatuses* is the plural that the option list offers, and it agrees with *were*.

    *Check.* Read the sentence with the singular: “The apparatus in the laboratory was replaced.” That is grammatical, so *apparatus* alone would need *was*, and the option list gives *were*. The only form that fits the verb is *apparatuses*.
  ],
  tier: "UTME STANDARD",
)

// ============================================================================
== 1.3 Pronouns

A pronoun stands in for a noun that has already been named. The paper tests two things about it: which *kind* it is, and which *case* it takes.

#concept-box("The five kinds of pronoun")[
  + *Personal:* I, you, he, she, it, we, they — and their object forms me, him, her, us, them.
  + *Possessive:* my, your, his, her, its, our, their (before a noun); mine, yours, his, hers, ours, theirs (standing alone).
  + *Reflexive:* myself, yourself, himself, herself, itself, ourselves, yourselves, themselves — used only when the subject and the object are the same person.
  + *Relative:* who, whom, whose, which, that — they introduce a clause and refer back to a noun.
  + *Demonstrative and indefinite:* this, that, these, those; someone, anybody, none, each, either.
]

#rule-box("Case: the pronoun's job decides its form")[
  + *Subject of the verb* → I, he, she, we, they, who. “*He* and *I* went to the market.”
  + *Object of a verb or a preposition* → me, him, her, us, them, whom. “The letter was addressed to *me*.” “Between you and *me*.”
  + *After than or as in a comparison* → complete the clause to decide. “She is taller than *I* (am)” but “She likes him more than (she likes) *me*.”
  + *Possessive before a gerund* → my, his, their. “*His* coming late annoyed the class”, not “Him coming late”.
]

The single most tested case question is the one with a preposition. *Between*, *with*, *for*, *to*, *from* and *except* are all prepositions, so what follows them is the object form — which is why “between you and I” is wrong even though it sounds formal, and “between you and me” is right even though it sounds casual.

#trap-box("Myself is not a polite I")[
  *Myself* is reflexive or emphatic: “I hurt *myself*” and “I did it *myself*”. It is not a substitute for *I* or *me* in a compound subject. “My brother and myself went” is wrong; “My brother and I went” is right. The test is simple: if the sentence would still make sense with the pronoun alone, the reflexive is wrong — you would not say “Myself went”.
]

// ============================================================================
== 1.4 Verbs — The Working Word

The verb is the only word a sentence cannot do without. It carries the tense, it decides whether the sentence has an object, and it fixes the form of every other word in the clause.

#diagram-fig("diagrams/ch01_verb_forms.svg",
  "The five forms of the verb. The paper tests the third form (he writes) through concord, and the fifth form (has written) through the perfect tense — both of which are covered in Chapters 2 and 3.", width: 100%)

#concept-box("Finite, non-finite and auxiliary")[
  + A *finite* verb carries tense and agrees with its subject: he *writes*, they *wrote*. Every sentence needs at least one.
  + A *non-finite* verb does not: the infinitive (*to write*), the participle (*writing*, *written*) and the gerund (*writing* as a noun).
  + An *auxiliary* helps the main verb: be, have, do, will, can, must, should. It is the auxiliary that carries the tense, which is why *has written* is present perfect while *written* alone has no tense at all.
  + A *transitive* verb takes an object (*kick the ball*); an *intransitive* verb does not (*sleep*, *arrive*). Only a transitive verb can be made passive.
]

*Regular and irregular.* A regular verb makes its past and its past participle by adding *-ed*: walk, walked, walked. An irregular verb changes its vowel or its ending: write, wrote, written; go, went, gone; take, took, taken; begin, began, begun. The three-column list is worth learning because the paper tests the participle far more often than the past tense.

#trap-box("Have went, has wrote, had took")[
  After *have*, *has* or *had* the verb must be the *past participle*, never the past tense. The correct forms are *has gone*, *has written*, *had taken*, *have begun*. The test is to say the three forms in order — go, went, gone — and then use the third one after the auxiliary.
]

#worked-example(
  3,
  "The auxiliary and the participle",
  [“By the time we arrived, the meeting \_\_\_.” Options: has begun, had begun, had began, has began.],
  [
    *Step 1 — fix the time.* *By the time we arrived* places the meeting before a past event, so the tense must be past perfect: *had* plus a participle.

    *Step 2 — choose the participle.* The three forms of *begin* are begin, began, begun. After *had* the form is *begun*, so *had began* is wrong.

    *Step 3 — check the auxiliary.* *Has* is present, and the sentence is past, so *has begun* cannot be right either.

    *Check.* The completed sentence reads “By the time we arrived, the meeting had begun.” Substitute the simple past — “the meeting began before we arrived” — and the meaning is the same, which confirms that the perfect is doing a past job and not a present one.
  ],
  tier: "UTME STANDARD",
)

// ============================================================================
== 1.5 Adjectives, Adverbs, Prepositions and Conjunctions

The last four classes are the ones that carry the sentence's detail: what a thing is like, how an action was done, where it happened, and how two ideas are joined.

#diagram-fig("diagrams/ch01_function_shift.svg",
  "The same word in three jobs. The class of a word is decided by the sentence, and this figure is the whole argument of the chapter in four lines.", width: 100%)

#concept-box("Adjective or adverb?")[
  An *adjective* describes a noun or a pronoun and answers *what kind? which one? how many?*: a *tall* boy, *three* books, *this* house. It sits before the noun or after a linking verb — *the soup is hot*.

  An *adverb* describes a verb, an adjective or another adverb, and answers *how? when? where? how often?*: he ran *quickly*, she arrived *late*, they meet *here*, he *always* wins. It is usually formed by adding *-ly* to the adjective, but not always: *fast*, *hard*, *late*, *well* and *soon* are adverbs with no -ly.

  The linking verbs are the ones that take an adjective, not an adverb: *be, seem, become, look, feel, taste, smell, sound, appear*. “The soup tastes *good*”, never “tastes well”.
]

#rule-box("Prepositions: point, surface, enclosure")[
  + *at* marks a point — at the door, at noon, at 10 a.m., at the bus stop.
  + *on* marks a surface or a day — on the table, on Monday, on the wall.
  + *in* marks an enclosure or a period — in the room, in June, in 2024, in the morning.
  + *to* marks direction — to Lagos, to the market; *into* marks movement to the inside; *onto* movement to the top.

  The ones tested most often are the fixed pairs, where the preposition belongs to the verb and cannot be changed: *depend on*, *insist on*, *consist of*, *comply with*, *prefer to*, *married to*, *capable of*, *interested in*, *afraid of*.
]

#rule-box("Conjunctions: coordinating and subordinating")[
  + *Coordinating* conjunctions join two equal parts. The seven are *for, and, nor, but, or, yet, so* — remembered as FANBOYS. They can join two independent clauses with a comma before them.
  + *Subordinating* conjunctions begin a dependent clause: *because, although, though, since, unless, until, when, while, if, after, before, as, that*. A subordinate clause cannot stand alone.
  + An *interjection* expresses feeling and stands outside the grammar of the sentence: *oh, alas, hmm, bravo, well*.
]

#worked-example(
  4,
  "Hard or hardly, late or lately",
  [“The students worked \_\_\_ and finished \_\_\_.” Options: hard / late, hardly / lately, hard / lately, hardly / late.],
  [
    *Step 1 — remember what the two forms mean.* *Hard* means with effort; *hardly* means almost not. *Late* means after the expected time; *lately* means recently. They are not comparative forms of each other.

    *Step 2 — fit the meaning.* Students who finish have worked with effort, so the first blank is *hard*. They finished after the expected time, so the second is *late*.

    *Step 3 — eliminate.* *Hardly* would mean they barely worked; *lately* would mean they finished recently, which does not pair with *finished*.

    *Check.* Replace each with its plain meaning and read the sentence: “The students worked with effort and finished after the expected time.” That is the meaning of the first option, so it is the key.
  ],
  tier: "UTME STANDARD",
)

#trap-box("Friendly, lovely, lonely, silly")[
  These end in -ly and are *adjectives*, because they describe a noun: a *friendly* teacher, a *lovely* day, a *lonely* road, a *silly* mistake. The adverb is formed with *in a … way* or with a different word altogether: *friendly* has no adverb in common use, so the paper offers it as the “not an adverb” option. The -ly test works from adjective to adverb, not backwards.
]

#worked-example(
  5,
  "When a word changes class",
  [In which of these sentences is the word *water* used as a verb? (a) The water is cold. (b) Please water the plants. (c) A water tank stands outside. (d) He drank a glass of water.],
  [
    *Step 1 — find what follows the word.* In (a) *water* is followed by the verb *is*, so *water* is the subject — a noun. In (d) it follows *of*, a preposition, so it is a noun. In (c) it stands before *tank* and describes it, so it is doing an adjective's job.

    *Step 2 — test (b).* *Water* follows *please* and takes the object *the plants*, and it could be put into the past — *watered the plants* — which is the mark of a verb.

    *Step 3 — confirm.* Only (b) allows a tense change on the word itself: “He watered the plants yesterday.” A noun cannot do that.

    *Check.* Ask what job the word is doing, never what the word usually is. The same word is a noun in three of the four sentences and a verb in one, and that is exactly why this question is set.
  ],
  tier: "JAMB CHALLENGE",
)

#diagram-fig("diagrams/recap_ch01.svg",
  "Chapter 1 recap card: the word classes.", width: 100%)

#block(width: 100%, fill: jamb-mint, stroke: 0.9pt + jamb-green, radius: 7pt, inset: 10pt)[
  #text(font: "DejaVu Sans", size: 9.2pt, weight: "bold", fill: jamb-green)[Chapter 1 in one breath]
  #v(3pt)
  #text(size: 10.1pt)[
    English has eight word classes, and a word's class is the job it is doing in that sentence: noun, pronoun, verb, adjective, adverb, preposition, conjunction, interjection. Nouns name and are proper, common, abstract, collective, count or mass — and the mass nouns (advice, furniture, information, equipment) have no plural at all. Pronouns take the case their job demands, so a preposition is followed by *me*, *him*, *them* and never by *I*, *he*, *they*, and *myself* is reserved for the subject that is also the object. Verbs have five forms, and after *have*, *has* or *had* the form is always the past participle. Adjectives describe nouns and adverbs describe everything else, but -ly is not a guarantee: friendly, lovely and silly are adjectives, while hard, fast, late and well are adverbs without it. Prepositions mark point, surface and enclosure; conjunctions are coordinating or subordinating, and only the seven coordinating ones can join two full clauses with a comma.
  ]
]

== 1.6 Chapter 1 in the Exam Hall — a Two-Minute Set

#mcq(1, [The word *courage* in the sentence “Courage is a rare quality” is], [a common noun], [an abstract noun], [a collective noun], [an adjective])
#mcq(2, [Which of the following is NOT an adverb?], [quickly], [hardly], [friendly], [well])
#mcq(3, [In the sentence “She has written three letters”, the word *written* is], [the past tense], [the present participle], [the base form], [the past participle])
#mcq(4, [The plural of *criterion* is], [criteria], [criterias], [criterions], [criterion])

#answer-key-grid(("B", "C", "D", "A"))

#answer-notes((
  ("B", [courage names a quality, not a thing you can touch or count, so it is an abstract noun. A common noun names a class of visible thing (boy, city); a collective noun names a group (team, jury).]),
  ("C", [friendly ends in -ly but describes a noun — a friendly teacher — so it is an adjective. Quickly, hardly and well all modify verbs.]),
  ("D", [after the auxiliary *has* the verb must be the past participle: write, wrote, written. The past tense *wrote* cannot follow *has*.]),
  ("A", [criterion is a Greek borrowing that keeps its Greek plural, criteria. Criterions is a spelling that does not exist, and criterion is the singular.]),
))

#chapter-checklist("Before you leave Chapter 1", (
  [Name the eight word classes without looking at the figure.],
  [Apply the article, -ly and position tests to a word you have not seen before.],
  [Give the plural of criterion, phenomenon, analysis and crisis.],
  [List the mass nouns that never take a plural.],
  [Choose between I and me after a preposition, and say why.],
  [Give the three forms of write, go, take and begin.],
))

#concept-box("Carry this into Chapter 2")[
  A singular subject and a singular verb have to agree, and the classes of Chapter 1 are what make that possible: the noun decides whether the verb takes -s, and the pronoun decides whether it takes *is* or *are*. Chapter 2 takes the same words and puts them together, which is where most of the marks in this paper are actually won.
]

